  "query_preview": "..\nFriday: An upper level trough over the area alongside colder air \nmoving in will lower high temperatures 4-6 degrees than seen on \nThursday. Winds in the afternoon will be breezy, with gusts up to 20 \nmph expected, especially in the deeper Basin, Waterville Plateau, \nCascade gaps, and Okanogan Valley. The air moving in remains dry, \nwith PWATs 80-90% of normal at around 0.5 inches, so no \nprecipitation is expected through the day. \n\nSaturday through Sunday: A deep low pressure system will swing down \ninto the area late Friday into Saturday, bringing a more appreciable \nchance for precipitation alongside a significant drop in \ntemperatures. Temperatures will further drop between Friday and \nSaturday, with highs expected in the low to mid 70s for much of the \narea. An important thing to keep an eye on will be sheltered valleys \nin the Idaho Panhandle and northern mountains. Models are indicating \nlow temperatures dropping into the mid-30s, which could lead to some \nfrost formation. Additionally, fog in these sheltered valleys is \npossible as well, with RHs remaining high due to that colder air \nstaying in the valleys. \n\nThis cold front will also bring a chance for rain across the \nmountains, with the northern mountains, highest elevations of the \nCascades, and the far southeastern portion of the ID Panhandle \nhaving a 20 percent chance and above at seeing 0.10 inches. There is \na 15 to 25 percent chance of thunderstorms in these same areas. With \nDCAPE values 500 J/kg or more throughout the areas, expect main \nthreats to be lightning and gusty outflow winds near 30 mph. Ahead \nof and with the cold frontal passage, winds will increase across the \nregion. Expect wind gusts 25-30 mph through much of the Basin, \nPalouse, Spokane/CdA, Waterville Plateau, and Wenatchee areas.\n\nMonday through Friday: As the low moves away from the area, slight \nridging will lead to an increase in temperatures and drier \nconditions for Monday and Tuesday. However, there is universal \nagreement in the clusters that another low pressure system will \ndrop down into the PNW coast Tuesday evening and remain there \nthrough the end of next work week. Mid and long term models are \nshowing better chances of more widespread precipitation, so stay\ntuned. The 6-10 and 8-14 Day Outlooks are also show that we \nhave higher chances for lower than normal temperatures and \nhigher than normal precipitation, so expect a major pattern \nchange over the weekend and next week.",
